## What happened

Starting in .NET 11, `DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS (and iOS/tvOS). Apple's native cryptographic frameworks (Security.framework / CryptoKit) have never supported DSA, and Microsoft finally removed the OpenSSL-based fallback on Apple platforms that earlier .NET versions used.

This is part of the long-running deprecation path — DSA was marked `[Obsolete]` on Apple platforms in .NET 7, and .NET 11 completed the removal.

## How to fix it

Since you only need **verification** of legacy signatures, your best options:

### Option 1 – Use BouncyCastle (recommended)

```csharp
// dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

bool VerifyDsa(byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] message, byte[] signature)
{
    var keyParam = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA-1withDSA");
    verifier.Init(forSigning: false, keyParam);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

This is cross-platform and requires no native dependencies.

### Option 2 – Use OpenSSL explicitly on macOS

Install OpenSSL via Homebrew and P/Invoke into it, but this adds deployment complexity.

### Option 3 – Runtime guard (if macOS verification isn't required)

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // ...
}
```

---

**TL;DR:** Apple never supported DSA natively; .NET 11 removed the shim. Use **BouncyCastle** for portable DSA signature verification.
